  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe main forecast concern will be on the severe weather potential \nthrough this evening. \n\nA complicated convective scenario is unfolding across the Great\nLakes region this afternoon. Preferred region of convective \ndevelopment has been across northeast Illinois along a corridor \nof low level warm/moist advection. This forcing is coincident \nwith strengthening upper low level flow and effective shear \nprofiles. Extreme instability with MLCAPES 4-5k J/kg CAPES is \nsituated across northeast Indiana with sharp eastward \ndiminishment in instability across northeast Indiana/northwest \nOhio. Cells across northeast Illinois are favoring strong cold \npools with signatures of very strong base velocity shifting \nsoutheast across far northwest Indiana. The combination of \nshallow low level southwest flow and deeper northwesterly flow \nin the mid/upper levels is favor some north to south preference \nin propagation vectors with the storms across northeast \nIllinois. Areas west of US Route 31 across northeast Indiana \nshould stand greatest risk of seeing higher end thunderstorm \nwind gusts (80+ mph) as the storms across NE Illinois drop \nsoutheast. In addition to the risk of higher end damaging wind \ngusts, the extreme instability will support some isolated large \nhail of 1 to 2 inches in diameter. Also, the aforementioned \nbackground shear profiles will continue to support some embedded\nsupercell structures with a risk of isolated tornadoes. 0-3km \nshear vectors oriented from the northwest at around 30 knots \ncould also support a few QLCS tornado spinups where any rear \ninflow jet can orient in a NW to SE fashion and where more \nmature cold pools develop.\n\nAdditional storms are expected to develop across the Great Lakes \nregion this evening as low level front pushes southward with an \nadditional risk of scattered damaging wind gusts through around 02Z. \nConfidence in severe gusts after 00Z does diminish somewhat however.\n\nBy Tuesday morning, the nose of the upper level jet streak will \nreach the Mid Atlantic states with lagging sfc trough/cool front \nexpected to extend from western PA to southern Illinois. Passage of \nthis front will lead quieter weather conditions through the middle \nof the week with temperatures/dew points closer to seasonable levels.\n\nThe next system to watch will be toward end of the work as the \npotential exists for synoptic wave to become convectively enhanced \nacross the Dakotas. Some question exists as to instability \nmagnitudes as far east as southern Great Lakes/Ohio Valley with this \nsystem with some indications of this system becoming more vertically \nstacked in nature well to the west.",
